  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when your curbless shower isn't working as it should, and leaks are definitely a top concern. Often, what looks like a simple curb removal is actually a much more complex floor and waterproofing rebuild. One of the biggest reasons for leaks comes down to poor slope. If your shower floor isn't pitched correctly, water can pool or even run out into the bathroom. We're talking about needing around a 1/4 inch per foot slope towards the drain to make sure water goes where it should. Another common culprit is insufficient waterproofing. It's not enough to just waterproof the shower area itself; a truly barrier-free entry needs waterproofing that extends beyond the shower zone to really keep things dry. Sometimes, it's also about the drain itself—if it's not sized correctly or placed optimally, you can run into issues. Addressing these issues usually means opening up the floor and really getting into the structure to fix the underlying problems. If you're dealing with leaks, it's a good idea to chat with a professional who understands the nuances of curbless design to get things properly assessed and corrected.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
